WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:06.099
So, widget-overrideable functions. We've talked a little bit about the widget editor itself.

2
00:00:06.099 --> 00:00:10.099
Let's go a bit more detail into the blueprint-y side of things.

3
00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:13.669
The key functions to be aware of.

4
00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:17.670
OnInitialize, Preconstruct, Construct, Tick and Destruct.

5
00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.670
You could get a long way with all of these really.

6
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:26.269
Preconstruct is a function we've talked a little bit about already.

7
00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:30.269
This essentially gets called as soon as our widget gets created.

8
00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.670
This is similar to Construct, which we'll talk about in a second.

9
00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:39.869
But this function, Preconstruct, gets called both at design time in the game and in editor.

10
00:00:39.869 --> 00:00:42.270
So, that has a lot in common with synchronized properties.

11
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.469
We've kind of already highlighted the discrepancy there,

12
00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:49.469
that Preconstruct is only for user widgets and downwards and is exposed to Blueprint.

13
00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:53.469
Synchronized properties is anything from UWidget downwards

14
00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:58.670
and it also has the burden of responsibility of funneling data down from the UWidget

15
00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.070
down to the SWidget.

16
00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:05.670
So, in any pre-existing Unreal widget, you'll see they have a synchronized properties value

17
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.670
and they are sending data down to the SWidget.

18
00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.670
So, it has additional stuff that it's doing.

19
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.670
But other than that, they have a lot in common there.

20
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:23.140
Yes, OnInitialized is called as the widget is created

21
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:28.140
and it has absolutely no correlation for whether or not the widget is actually displayed.

22
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.140
So, an important thing about Construct and Preconstruct

23
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:37.140
is that these will be called when our widget actually gets put onto screen.

24
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:42.140
So, Construct is kind of the closest thing to an active begin play function.

25
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.140
It's kind of like our widget has started.

26
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:50.140
But a kind of important caveat is that it's very typical that Construct might get called multiple times.

27
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:54.140
So, every time you add a widget to Viewport,

28
00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:57.140
it's Construct and it's Preconstruct will get called.

29
00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.140
Every time you remove a widget from the Viewport,

30
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.140
we get a Destruct get called.

31
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:05.140
So, this is kind of like equivalent-ish to our end play.

32
00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:10.139
But again, a widget that gets added to screen or removed from screen multiple times

33
00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:13.139
will have Construct and Destruct called multiple times.

34
00:02:13.139 --> 00:02:18.580
If you really want something a little bit closer to the real functionality of begin play,

35
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.580
which is this thing gets called once,

36
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.580
with the caveat that in multiplayer scenarios,

37
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.580
with things becoming out of relevancy, begin play might get called multiple times.

38
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.580
So, I'm slightly flipping there.

39
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.580
The onInitialize is a function that gets called only once.

40
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:37.580
And it has absolutely no bearing on whether or not that widget gets displayed,

41
00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:38.580
a widget could never get added to screen.

42
00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.580
It's onInitialize that gets called the moment that widget gets created.

43
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:47.580
It's important to mention that onInitialize is called very, very early in the process.

44
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:53.580
So early, in fact, that if you have variables that are marked as exposed on spawn,

45
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:59.580
those variables won't even have their exposed on spawn value set yet on Initialize.

46
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.580
If that's a little bit confusing, I'm not completely sure what I mean by that.

47
00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.580
Let's actually just do a quick demonstration.

48
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:10.770
So, if we go to our widget, this is just our new widget.

49
00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.770
I'm just going to delete all the content we put in there,

50
00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.770
because we just don't need it.

51
00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:18.770
We can leave the visuals, that doesn't matter too much.

52
00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:27.129
So, if we do our pre-construct, we do a print string here,

53
00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:30.129
you can see we've got the bool for whether or not we're in design time.

54
00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:38.520
We're going to do our construct, and we're going to say pre-construct here,

55
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:58.240
construct here, destruct, you can see where this is going, and initialize.

56
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:14.159
Okay, so, if we were to add this widget to our screen,

57
00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:17.089
you can see all of those things will get called.

58
00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:21.089
I've got a kind of server setup running here, let me just go into standalone.

59
00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:25.139
What I'm going to do is I'm actually going to do begin play,

60
00:04:25.139 --> 00:04:28.139
I'm going to promote that to a variable,

61
00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:34.189
and what I'll do is I'll just quickly put in a debug key here.

62
00:04:34.189 --> 00:04:38.189
So, debug key one, and I'm just going to do a flip-flop.

63
00:04:38.189 --> 00:04:40.189
Very useful in control flow function,

64
00:04:40.189 --> 00:04:42.189
but you're just quickly trying to test things.

65
00:04:42.189 --> 00:04:49.149
And so, on the flip, we're going to put the widget on screen,

66
00:04:49.149 --> 00:04:54.850
on flop, these are not official terms.

67
00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:56.850
We're going to remove it from parent,

68
00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.069
we're just going to remove it from the screen.

69
00:04:59.069 --> 00:05:02.069
So, let's just see what happens.

70
00:05:02.069 --> 00:05:06.300
So, when we begin play, you see on initialize gets called,

71
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.300
and then I added the screen, pre-construct and then construct get called,

72
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.300
so obviously the kind of lower one is being done first,

73
00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.300
you can see pre-construct is getting called first, destruct is called,

74
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.360
add it again, pre-construct and construct.

75
00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.360
The object isn't getting deleted, it's not changing,

76
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.360
but it's getting called multiple times, right?

77
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.360
But the other element to kind of bear in mind here,

78
00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.740
again, initialize only getting called once,

79
00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.740
is that if you do expose on spawn,

80
00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.740
so you might not be familiar with this,

81
00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:37.740
but this is a concept not unique to UMG, but it's also for any actors.

82
00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.740
If you have some data that you kind of want to be,

83
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.740
you know, something you can set as that thing gets created,

84
00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.740
I'm just going to do like some string,

85
00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.970
and mark it instance editable and expose on spawn,

86
00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.970
you'll see that when I go to my level blueprint,

87
00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:59.470
if something is marked as expose on spawn,

88
00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.470
if I refresh this node,

89
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:05.470
you can see I now get to pass in some value for some string to be,

90
00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:10.470
so I'm just going to type in some cool phrase,

91
00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:16.660
and if we print that on our construct,

92
00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:21.660
instead of saying construct, we print what the cool phrase is,

93
00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:25.379
or even our pre-construct,

94
00:06:25.379 --> 00:06:33.879
when I press one, they both say some cool phrase,

95
00:06:33.879 --> 00:06:36.879
so clearly, that value has been passed in.

96
00:06:36.879 --> 00:06:45.500
However, if I were to do the same thing with our initialize,

97
00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.500
it's, well, actually, what I'm going to do is I'm going to give it some initial value,

98
00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.500
because otherwise you're going to see nothing,

99
00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:52.500
so I'm just going to do ee,

100
00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:58.769
and when I play, you see it says ee,

101
00:06:58.769 --> 00:07:03.769
and that's because initialize is called so early in the flow

102
00:07:03.769 --> 00:07:07.769
that it's before our,

103
00:07:07.769 --> 00:07:10.769
even before our expose on spawn variables

104
00:07:10.769 --> 00:07:13.769
have been kind of properly assigned,

105
00:07:13.769 --> 00:07:15.769
so just an important thing to bear in mind

106
00:07:15.769 --> 00:07:17.769
when you're working with some of this stuff

107
00:07:17.769 --> 00:07:19.769
is that initialize is very powerful,

108
00:07:19.769 --> 00:07:22.769
but it's literally, you should really only be setting internal data.

109
00:07:22.769 --> 00:07:25.769
You can't rely on data necessarily coming into the widget

110
00:07:25.769 --> 00:07:28.769
because it's called so early

111
00:07:28.769 --> 00:07:30.769
that that data may not even be valid yet.

112
00:07:30.769 --> 00:07:32.769
Just a little thing to bear in mind.

113
00:07:32.769 --> 00:07:36.470
And then the last element here is tick,

114
00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:40.470
which is very synonymous with this tick we're already kind of used to.

115
00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.470
This is a thing that's called once per frame.

116
00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:47.470
Unlike actor tick, same as construct,

117
00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:50.470
this tick is only called whilst that widget is on screen,

118
00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.470
so if I can just quickly demonstrate that.

119
00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:59.620
If we go to our widget tick,

120
00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:07.230
I'm just going to say hello because I'm lazy.

121
00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:11.949
You can see it's peering lots.

122
00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:14.949
I take it off screen, and it's not printing anymore,

123
00:08:14.949 --> 00:08:17.949
and I put it back on screen, and it's printing a lot again.

124
00:08:17.949 --> 00:08:24.079
If you're wondering where that other hello came from,

125
00:08:24.079 --> 00:08:25.079
that was actually our initialize,

126
00:08:25.079 --> 00:08:29.399
so I'll, yeah, that wasn't the tick for having once.

127
00:08:29.399 --> 00:08:32.399
So, yeah, that's kind of everything

128
00:08:32.399 --> 00:08:35.399
about the kind of initial core functions,

129
00:08:35.399 --> 00:08:37.399
core events to kind of know about.

130
00:08:37.399 --> 00:08:40.399
Obviously, these all have C++ equivalents,

131
00:08:40.399 --> 00:08:42.399
so these are all called native on initialize,

132
00:08:42.399 --> 00:08:44.399
native on reconstruct, native reconstruct,

133
00:08:44.399 --> 00:08:46.399
native construct, native tick, native destruct,

134
00:08:46.399 --> 00:08:50.399
so do exactly the same things but in C++.

135
00:08:50.399 --> 00:08:52.820
Then we've got some input functions.

136
00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:56.820
This is kind of just a small subset of what's available,

137
00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:58.820
but we'll just kind of quickly discuss some of them.

138
00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:02.820
So we have mouse put button up and down,

139
00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:04.820
so down is quite self-explanatory

140
00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.820
as the button goes down, so as you click in,

141
00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:11.820
mouse button up or key up is as these buttons go up,

142
00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:13.820
and they pass in some kind of data

143
00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:16.820
about what the widget's current geometry is,

144
00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:19.820
information about the mouse or button event,

145
00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:21.820
so what key was clicked,

146
00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.820
where was your cursor on screen, that kind of thing.

147
00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.820
A couple of important things to bear in mind

148
00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.820
is that mouse button events

149
00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.820
will probably only be called when the cursor is on,

150
00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.820
so there's a variable you can set in the player controller

151
00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.820
to say show mouse cursor,

152
00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:41.820
but also they'll only trigger

153
00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.820
when there is actually kind of physical widget geometry

154
00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:47.820
under the cursor,

155
00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:50.820
so even if you set this widget as a focus,

156
00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.820
the mouse events will only be viable

157
00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.820
when the mouse is intersecting visible,

158
00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:00.820
and I'm not talking about visible as in the player can see it,

159
00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.820
I mean with the visibility setting of visible,

160
00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.820
i.e. they can be hit by tracing,

161
00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:08.820
to make it completely clear what I mean,

162
00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.820
whenever you're dealing with widgets,

163
00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:12.820
there is a visibility setting here,

164
00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:16.320
and we have a couple of different settings,

165
00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:18.320
we have not hit testable,

166
00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.320
which is the kind of standard setting

167
00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:22.320
for things that might contain other things,

168
00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.320
but shouldn't be hittable themselves,

169
00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:26.320
either from navigation or from clicking,

170
00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:29.320
we have hidden, so it occupies space,

171
00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.320
but you can't see it,

172
00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.320
collapsed, which means it doesn't occupy space

173
00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:33.320
and you can't see it,

174
00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:34.320
and last of all we have visible,

175
00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.320
which means this thing does occupy space,

176
00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.320
you can see it,

177
00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.320
and it can be hit,

178
00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:41.320
either by trying to navigate around the screen

179
00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.320
with your gamepad or your keyboard,

180
00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.320
and it can also be clicked on,

181
00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:49.320
so it's only when your cursor is over something that is visible,

182
00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:52.320
that is part of that widget,

183
00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.320
that your mouse events will get called,

184
00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:58.320
sorry, accidentally opening applications there,

185
00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.320
we have onMouseMove,

186
00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:02.320
so that's whilst your mouse is moving

187
00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.320
within geometry of that widget,

188
00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.320
onKeyDown does the same thing,

189
00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:08.320
it doesn't have that same requirement

190
00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:12.320
for the visible geometry,

191
00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.320
but onKeyDown likely won't get called in your widget

192
00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:18.320
unless your widget is the focus of a player,

193
00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:21.320
we'll talk a little bit about focus in a little bit,

194
00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:24.320
but the short explanation is that

195
00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:28.320
when you want something to receive,

196
00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.320
keyboard or gamepad input,

197
00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.320
the easiest thing to do is set that widget

198
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.320
to be the focus of that player controller,

199
00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.320
which you can just do through a very easy function,

200
00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:39.320
you can just go like this,

201
00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:41.320
I put a widget on screen,

202
00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:44.320
and I just say setFocus,

203
00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.320
and that's the kind of simplest way to do it,

204
00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.320
there are other kind of caveats to bear in mind,

205
00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.320
but I have now set this widget to be focused,

206
00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:53.320
that widget could then set children of it

207
00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:55.320
to be focused as well,

208
00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:58.320
but that's the easiest way to say that

209
00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.320
now this can probably receive,

210
00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:02.320
the only kind of other thing to bear in mind

211
00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.320
is that widget actually has to be marked as focusable,

212
00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:07.320
so if I went to my new widget,

213
00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:11.509
you would need to go to the designer,

214
00:12:11.509 --> 00:12:12.509
to a hierarchy here,

215
00:12:12.509 --> 00:12:15.509
and just make sure that it is actually a focusable widget,

216
00:12:15.509 --> 00:12:17.509
so you just need this all ticked,

217
00:12:17.509 --> 00:12:23.509
and now that object is likely to receive onKeyDown

218
00:12:23.509 --> 00:12:30.179
and functions onKeyDown, onKeyUp,

219
00:12:30.179 --> 00:12:31.179
and then onPreviewKeyDown,

220
00:12:31.179 --> 00:12:35.179
so this is, whereas there is a mouse button down,

221
00:12:35.179 --> 00:12:38.179
mouse button up, key down, key up,

222
00:12:38.179 --> 00:12:41.179
previewKeyDown only exists for key down,

223
00:12:41.179 --> 00:12:43.179
there is no preview mouse down,

224
00:12:43.179 --> 00:12:45.179
there is no preview key up,

225
00:12:45.179 --> 00:12:47.179
it's only for key down,

226
00:12:47.179 --> 00:12:50.179
and whereas the standard functionality

227
00:12:50.179 --> 00:12:51.179
for all of these input events

228
00:12:51.179 --> 00:12:55.179
is input kind of funnels to the deepest,

229
00:12:55.179 --> 00:12:59.179
most focused widget that it possibly can,

230
00:12:59.179 --> 00:13:04.179
and if that widget can then essentially respond to that event,

231
00:13:04.179 --> 00:13:06.179
you can see we can return either a handled

232
00:13:06.179 --> 00:13:09.179
or an unhandled response to this input to say,

233
00:13:09.179 --> 00:13:11.179
oh, I did something with this input

234
00:13:11.179 --> 00:13:13.179
or I didn't do something with this input,

235
00:13:13.179 --> 00:13:15.179
it doesn't matter if you actually did,

236
00:13:15.179 --> 00:13:17.179
but this is just saying to the system,

237
00:13:17.179 --> 00:13:19.179
oh, maybe you should look elsewhere

238
00:13:19.179 --> 00:13:21.179
for someone else who can deal with this,

239
00:13:21.179 --> 00:13:22.179
this is essentially saying,

240
00:13:22.179 --> 00:13:24.179
you know, look elsewhere or don't,

241
00:13:24.179 --> 00:13:25.179
handled means I've done it,

242
00:13:25.179 --> 00:13:27.179
you don't need to look any further,

243
00:13:27.179 --> 00:13:28.179
unhandled means no,

244
00:13:28.179 --> 00:13:30.179
maybe kind of bubble this up the chain,

245
00:13:30.179 --> 00:13:31.179
and so what that will do is

246
00:13:31.179 --> 00:13:33.179
the input will go to the deepest,

247
00:13:33.179 --> 00:13:34.179
most focused widget,

248
00:13:34.179 --> 00:13:38.179
and if the input is unhandled,

249
00:13:38.179 --> 00:13:41.179
then naturally the input will filter up

250
00:13:41.179 --> 00:13:43.179
the actual widget hierarchy

251
